  vetChecklist: [
    'Screenshot of the REPS entry for the exact operating site showing \'cirugía plástica y estética\' (or the relevant surgical service) and a licensed operating room under installed capacity.',
    'Screenshot of the surgeon\'s ReTHUS record showing the surgical specialty and no sanctions, matched to the cédula number on the consent form.',
    'The surgeon\'s SCCP membership (or equivalent specialty society for non-plastic procedures) and the university that granted the specialty title.',
    'Name and ReTHUS record of the anesthesiologist who will be present for the whole operation.',
    'The name of the receiving hospital for emergencies, in writing, and its distance from the clinic and from your lodging.',
    'An itemised quote in the clinic\'s legal name listing every inclusion and exclusion, the cancellation policy and who pays for treating complications.',
    'Written confirmation that only one procedure (or a stated, limited combination) will be done, with the planned anesthesia time.',
    'For implants or grafts: brand, model and serial numbers and that you will receive the implant card; for BBL, written confirmation of subcutaneous-only injection.',
    'The recovery lodging\'s name, address, on-site nursing arrangement and night contact for the surgeon.',
    'A commitment to provide the operative report, anesthesia record, pathology and discharge instructions in English before you fly, and the date of your last in-person check.',
  ],
  faqs: [
    { question: 'How many Americans actually go to Colombia for medical care?', answer: 'Colombia recorded 26,786 non-resident foreigners entering for health reasons in 2025, and Anato puts the US at about 41% of health visitors, so roughly 11,000 Americans a year by that measure. Migración Colombia counts self-declared motives, so the true number is higher.' },
    { question: 'Is plastic surgery in Colombia safe?', answer: 'Not as a category. Antioquia\'s health secretary counted 11 cosmetic-procedure deaths in Medellín in 2024, five in 2025 and one by May 2026, and foreign patients, including an NYPD detective in Cali in October 2025, are among the dead. Risk drops sharply with a ReTHUS-registered plastic surgeon, a REPS-licensed operating room, an anesthesiologist and one procedure at a time.' },
    { question: 'How do I check a Colombian surgeon\'s licence?', answer: 'Search the SISPRO ReTHUS page by name or cédula; it shows profession, registered specialty, authorizing entity and sanctions. The Ministry says practicing a surgical specialty not recorded in ReTHUS is illegal. Then confirm membership at the SCCP\'s \'Buscar Cirujano\' directory.' },
    { question: 'How do I check whether a clinic is licensed?', answer: 'Use REPS at prestadores.minsalud.gov.co/habilitacion: open \'Registro Actual\', search the provider, then check \'Servicios\' for \'cirugía plástica y estética\' and \'Capacidad instalada\' for an operating room at the exact site. A clinic licensed only for consultations may not operate.' },
    { question: 'What is the difference between a plastic surgeon and a \'cirujano estético\'?', answer: 'Colombia recognises one specialty, cirugía plástica, estética y reconstructiva, which requires a postgraduate surgical residency. \'Medicina estética\' and \'cirugía estética\' are not recognised specialties and are often taught in short courses. General physicians and non-doctors have performed liposuction and gluteal injections in several fatal cases.' },
    { question: 'How much does a BBL or liposuction cost in Colombia versus the US?', answer: 'A Medellín reference list puts liposuction at COP 8 to 18 million (about US$1,900 to 4,300) and BBL at COP 12 to 25 million (about US$2,900 to 6,000) with surgeon, anesthesia and operating room included. ASPS average US surgeon fees alone are US$4,711 for liposuction and US$7,264 for fat-graft buttock augmentation.' },
    { question: 'Why are \'combo\' packages dangerous?', answer: 'Cali\'s health secretary, the SCCP and Centro Médico Imbanaco have warned that risk of complications and death rises with time under anesthesia, and several foreign deaths in Cali followed three procedures in one operation. Ask for one procedure per anesthetic and at least several weeks between surgeries.' },
    { question: 'Are stem cell treatments in Medellín legal and approved?', answer: 'They are not FDA approved, and Colombia has no general authorization for stem cell therapy outside clinical trials and established blood-disorder uses. INVIMA has approved specific trials, and in 2026 Supersalud found \'stem cell\' offers in Bogotá that were actually banned biopolymers. Treat them as experimental.' },
    { question: 'Can I use my HSA or deduct the cost?', answer: 'Elective cosmetic surgery is not a qualified medical expense under IRS Publication 502 unless it corrects a deformity, in Colombia or anywhere. Medically necessary care abroad, such as dental implants or bariatric surgery, is treated like domestic care for the deduction (above 7.5% of adjusted gross income) and HSA purposes.' },
    { question: 'Will my US insurance cover complications?', answer: 'Generally no for cosmetic surgery and its complications; ASPS notes that most plans exclude them and that complications after surgery abroad are often paid by Medicaid or the patient. Emergency care at a US hospital will still be provided, but revision surgery is at your cost.' },
    { question: 'When can I fly home after surgery?', answer: 'CDC advises no flying for 10 days after chest or abdominal surgery, which covers liposuction of the trunk, tummy tuck, BBL, breast and bariatric surgery, and waiting 7 to 10 days after facial, eyelid or nose procedures. Buy a changeable ticket.' },
    { question: 'Is Medellín safe for a solo medical traveler?', answer: 'The State Department rates Colombia Level 3, Reconsider Travel, and the US Embassy has issued alerts in January 2024 and July 2026 about drugging and robbery of foreigners met through dating apps. Medellín logged 428 drugging robberies in 2026 to August, most in El Poblado and Laureles. Bring a companion, skip dating apps, and use clinic drivers.' },
    { question: 'Which parts of Colombia does the US say not to visit?', answer: 'As of the 31 March 2026 advisory: Arauca, Cauca except Popayán, Valle del Cauca except Cali, Norte de Santander and within 10 km of the Venezuelan border are Level 4, Do Not Travel. Bogotá, Medellín, Cali, Barranquilla and Cartagena are not excluded but fall under the national Level 3.' },
    { question: 'What can I do if something goes wrong in Colombia?', answer: 'Report the clinic to the city health secretariat, which can inspect and seal it; file a free PQRD complaint with Supersalud through the SuperArgo portal; and file a criminal complaint with the Fiscalía if there was harm. Civil damages need a Colombian lawyer; US courts rarely have jurisdiction.' },
    { question: 'Do I need a visa or any form to enter Colombia?', answer: 'US citizens enter visa-free for up to 90 days with a passport valid for the stay and an onward ticket. Migración Colombia\'s CheckMig pre-registration can be completed 72 to 1 hours before each flight; it speeds entry and some airlines request it.' },
  ],
